  "query_preview": "/SUNDAY NIGHT THROUGH FRIDAY/...\nIssued at 304 PM EDT Fri Aug 21 2026\n\nA drier and marginally cooler air mass will spill into the Ohio \nValley from the northwest behind the cold front on Sunday as mid- \nand upper-level troughing sets up east of the Mississippi. This air \nmass, supported by 1018 mb sfc high pressure, should remain in place \nover the region into Monday, with sub-1\" PWATs indicating moisture \nlevels around the 10th percentile of model climatology. It should be \nrather comfortable for late August during this time period, with \nhighs in the upper 70s and low-to-mid 80s and lows in the upper 50s \nand low 60s.\n\nThe primary trough axis over the eastern CONUS will begin to eject \nto the east Monday night into Tuesday as upper ridging over the \nwestern CONUS spreads toward the lower Miss. Valley. This should \nallow a sfc warm front to creep to the NE next Tuesday, with greater \nmoisture located in the vicinity of the front. If the front remains \nSW of the area, as is currently the most likely solution, dry \nweather should continue into the middle of next week. However, if \nthe ridge/warm front can make it closer to the area, low-end chances \nfor showers and storms may return by next Wednesday. Next Wednesday \ninto Thursday, another upper shortwave is expected to descend on the \nNE side of the ridge across the upper Midwest. This may bring a weak \nfront toward the area, prompting a return of shower/storm chances, \nthough forecast confidence in storms is low at this time. \nTemperatures should gradually increase by the second half of next \nweek, returning to near climatological normals by next Wednesday and \nThursday.",
